# PHASE 4 — RESOURCE ECONOMY AND DEPLETION

Resources must have actual quantities.

Gathering must consume resources, and depleted resources must become unavailable.

## Example initial resource quantities

| Resource | Example quantity |
|---|---:|
| Twig | 5 wood |
| Small tree | 10 wood |
| Medium tree | 12 wood |
| Large tree | 15 wood |

These are example balancing values. Make them configurable rather than hardcoding them throughout the project.

## Resource mechanics

- Every resource node has a quantity.
- Gathering decreases the quantity.
- Gathering cannot exceed the available quantity.
- Depleted nodes cannot provide more resources.
- Resource nodes should visually reflect depletion where supported.
- Resources must be transferred into NPC inventories or storage.
- Gathering requires time and appropriate tools where applicable.
- Resource gathering should account for distance and efficiency.
- Regeneration must follow defined rules.
- Renewable and nonrenewable resources must behave differently.

NPCs must recognize when an area is depleted.

They should consider:

- Exploring other areas.
- Conserving resources.
- Using alternative materials.
- Sharing available resources.
- Prioritizing essential construction.

Do not automatically replenish depleted resources.

Resource availability must influence the entire village economy.

