# PHASE 10 — MULTIPLAYER SIMULATION BALANCING

Multiplayer must preserve the simulation's natural progression.

## 10.1 Remove multiplayer speedup controls

Do not include time-speedup buttons or controls in multiplayer.

Players should not be able to independently accelerate the shared simulation.

The world must operate on a consistent synchronized simulation clock.

## 10.2 Balance progression without speed controls

Because multiplayer does not have speedup functionality, optimize progression so players remain engaged.

Do this through:

- More efficient NPC task scheduling.
- Better distribution of community responsibilities.
- Reduced unnecessary idle time.
- More meaningful simultaneous activities.
- Appropriate resource availability.
- Balanced construction durations.
- More responsive social interactions.
- Properly tuned learning rates.

Do not simply make all systems excessively fast.

Preserve the slower cognitive development and meaningful civilization progression.

The goal is to make multiplayer engaging without sacrificing simulation realism.

## 10.3 Synchronization

Ensure that:

- NPC positions are synchronized.
- Relationship changes are synchronized.
- Construction stages are synchronized.
- Resource quantities are authoritative.
- Migration events are consistent.
- Social Log entries are not duplicated.
- Governance decisions are consistent.
- Simulation time is controlled by the shared world state.

Prevent multiple clients from independently executing the same NPC action.

Use the existing authoritative multiplayer architecture where possible.

